.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt ZFS-SNAPSHOT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs-snapshot
.Nd take today's dated snapshot of a dataset and expire old ones
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Dnrv
.Op Fl p Ar profile
.Op Fl t Ar date
.Ar dataset
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is meant to run once a day, from
.Xr cron 8 .
It takes the snapshot
.Ar dataset Ns @ Ns Ar YYYY-MM-DD
for today, then destroys the snapshot taken a week ago, unless that
day was a Sunday, and every snapshot six months old.
So a weekday's snapshot lives a week, and a Sunday's six months.
.Pp
Six months before is taken with the day clamped to the length of the
month it lands in: 31 August less six months is 28 February.
Consecutive days therefore do not map to consecutive days: 2026-02-28
maps to 2025-08-28, and 2026-03-01 to 2025-09-01.
Each run destroys every date the six-month mark has moved past since
the day before, not one date, so no snapshot falls between two runs.
.Pp
The snapshot comes first, and when it fails nothing is destroyed, so an
old snapshot never goes before its replacement exists.
A snapshot already taken today is not an error, and the expiry still
runs, so running again is harmless.
A snapshot is destroyed only when it exists; a destroy that fails does
not stop the others.
Dates are worked out by arithmetic, not by
.Xr date 1 ,
which is asked for nothing but today.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-p profile"
.It Fl D
Report the commands run and the snapshots there were none of to
destroy, as well as what
.Fl v
reports.
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl n
A dry run: show each
.Xr zfs 8
command that would change something, and run none.
.It Fl p Ar profile
The snapshotting profile; the only one, and the default, is
.Ql default ,
the rule above.
.It Fl r
Take and destroy the snapshots recursively, the dataset's descendants
with it.
.It Fl t Ar date
Act as if today were
.Ar date ,
given as
.Ar YYYY-MM-DD ,
for instance to replay a day the job did not run.
.It Fl V
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl v
Report each snapshot created and destroyed.
.El
.Pp
Reports go to stderr.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev PATH
.Pa /sbin
and
.Pa /usr/sbin
are added at its end, where
.Xr zfs 8
lives, since a Linux cron job's
.Ev PATH
and an unprivileged Linux user's leave them out.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Today's snapshot exists, and every snapshot due was destroyed.
.It 1
The dataset does not exist, or the snapshot could not be taken, and
nothing was destroyed; or a snapshot due could not be destroyed, after
the others were tried.
.It 2
A usage error: a profile other than
.Ql default ,
or a
.Fl t
that is not a date, among them.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The daily job, in root's crontab:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
15 3 * * * zfs-snapshot -r tank/home
.Ed
.Pp
What the run of 1 March 2026 would do, done again by hand:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zfs-snapshot -n -t 2026-03-01 tank/home
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfs-prune-snapshots 1 ,
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zfs-destroy 8 ,
.Xr zfs-snapshot 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Each run expires only what fell due since the day before: a day the
job did not run leaves that day's expiries undone, and
.Ql zfs-snapshot -t YYYY-MM-DD dataset
replays it.
.Pp
OpenZFS has a page of the same name in section 8, for
.Ql zfs snapshot ;
.Ql man 1 zfs-snapshot
names this one.
